<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
   Real backup rules (ROADMAP Task 15 Phase B), replacing the Android Studio sample template that
   shipped here untouched (every rule commented out) since the project's first commit.

   Governs Android's legacy Auto Backup-to-Google-Drive mechanism on API 23-30, superseded on
   API 31+ by data_extraction_rules.xml (see that file). minSdk is 28 (app/build.gradle.kts), so
   this file still has real API 28-30 devices to apply to. At those API levels there is only ONE
   backup channel: Auto Backup to Drive. There is no separate on-device "device transfer" concept
   to weigh differently the way data_extraction_rules.xml can; that split only exists from API 31
   onward, so every rule below applies uniformly, the same way cloud-backup does in that file.

   AGENTS.md §1: "Offline-first, single local SQLite database, zero required external cloud sync or
   accounts." Until this file was touched, android:allowBackup="true" plus these all-commented-out
   sample rules meant Auto Backup was silently sweeping the ENTIRE app-private directory, Room's
   database AND the content-addressed cover images, to Google Drive on every eligible device,
   size-capped and not restorable on demand. That contradicts the local-first premise more
   severely than the log file this task was originally scoped around: the app already ships its
   own explicit, user-driven alternatives with none of that opacity, `.sqlite` backup/restore
   and CSV export (ROADMAP Task 8), both opt-in, both local until the user themselves picks a
   destination via SAF. Auto Backup adds nothing those don't already cover better, while routing
   the user's reading library through a cloud account this app's premise says it should never
   need. So the fix here isn't a narrow log carve-out; it's excluding everything this app
   actually writes to app-private storage, full stop.

   Result: every domain this app writes to is excluded below, so this channel ends up
   transferring nothing. android:allowBackup stays "true" in AndroidManifest.xml rather than
   flipping to "false" outright; see that file's own comment for why (short version:
   data_extraction_rules.xml's API 31+ device-transfer channel, which this app deliberately
   keeps enabled, is gated by the same manifest attribute, so disabling it here would take that
   channel down too on every API level, not just this one).
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <!-- The whole SQLite "databases" directory (Context.getDatabasePath's parent): media_tracker.db,
         its -wal/-shm sidecars, the backup-staging *.tmp file DefaultDatabaseBackupUseCase writes
         beside it, and the *.pre-restore-bak / *.restore-result marker files
         DatabaseRestoreRecovery leaves beside it during a restore. One rule for the whole
         directory (path="." recurses) rather than naming each file individually, so a future file
         written next to the database is excluded by construction instead of by remembering to
         update this list. -->
    <!-- The app's data directory root itself. Excluding "file", "database" and "sharedpref" below
         does NOT cover this: "root" is a separate domain covering files written directly into the
         data directory rather than into one of its known subdirectories. Without this line, a file
         that ends up there (by this app, or by a library it depends on) would still be swept to
         Drive, leaving a gap in what is otherwise a deliberately complete exclusion. -->
    <exclude domain="root" path="." />

    <exclude domain="database" path="." />

    <!-- The whole app-private files directory (Context.filesDir): today this holds `covers/`
         (content-addressed cover images, AGENTS.md §4) and will hold `logs/` (ROADMAP Task 15
         Phase B's persistent log store, a capped file at `<filesDir>/logs/`) once that phase
         lands. Excluding the whole domain, not just those two named subdirectories, is a
         deliberate fail-closed choice: a new subdirectory added under filesDir later is excluded
         automatically, rather than silently defaulting to "backed up" until someone remembers to
         list it here too. Nothing else is currently written under filesDir, so this has no
         narrower correct scope today anyway. -->
    <exclude domain="file" path="." />

    <!-- Not used by this app today (no SharedPreferences call site anywhere in the codebase;
         settings live in Room's app_settings table instead, see ROADMAP Task 7). Excluded
         defensively rather than left to the default "backed up" behavior: a future dependency
         (an AndroidX library, a future feature) that starts writing preferences should default to
         staying off Drive too, consistent with the fail-closed choice above for the files
         directory, not require remembering to come back and add this line then. -->
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
</full-backup-content>
